  "title": "PCI DSS architecture: 12 requirements that matter",
  "content": "It's the kind of problem that's easy to reproduce and hard to explain. versioning evolving is the kind of decision that looks reversible until it isn't. Here's the root cause, not just the symptom.\n\nPCI DSS is often treated as a compliance checkbox: scan the network, fill out the SAQ, get the certificate. But the standard's 12 requirements encode a set of architectural constraints that, if you ignore them, force painful rework later. The requirements that actually shape how payment systems get built are the ones about network segmentation, key management, logging, and secure development. This post walks through those constraints as design drivers, not audit items, and shows how a typical cardholder data environment (CDE) gets structured around them. The concrete problem: teams often discover segmentation and logging gaps only after a penetration test or QSA review, when the cost to fix is highest.\n\n## The problem this solves\n\nA payment system that touches card data — even if it only stores a token or passes a PAN through — inherits PCI DSS scope. Scope is the enemy: the more systems in scope, the more controls you must apply, the more evidence you must produce, and the more ways an auditor can find a gap. The standard itself is public and detailed, but the architectural implications are buried in requirement language that reads like policy. The goal here is to translate the requirements that drive architecture into concrete decisions: where to put the CDE boundary, how to handle keys, what to log, and how to keep developers out of production data.\n\n## Prerequisites and what you'll build\n\nYou should be comfortable with VPC networking, IAM, and basic cryptography concepts. The examples use AWS service names because they are widely documented, but the patterns apply to any cloud. You will not build a full payment gateway here; instead you will build the skeleton of a CDE: a segmented network, a key management flow, a logging pipeline, and a secure CI/CD gate. Each step maps to one or more PCI DSS requirements.\n\n## Step 1 — Draw the CDE boundary before you draw anything else\n\nPCI DSS Requirement 1 is about network security controls. The architectural consequence: you need a clearly defined boundary around systems that store, process, or transmit cardholder data. In AWS, that typically means a separate VPC or a set of subnets with strict security groups and NACLs, plus a firewall or security group ruleset that denies all traffic except explicitly required flows.\n\nA common mistake is to put the payment service in the same VPC as the rest of the application and rely on application-level auth. That expands scope to every instance in the VPC. Instead, create a dedicated VPC for the CDE, peer it to the application VPC only through a narrow set of endpoints, and use AWS PrivateLink or a transit gateway with route table isolation. The goal is that a compromise of the general application tier does not grant network access to the CDE.\n\nWhy this matters for architecture: once the boundary exists, you can apply stricter controls inside it — no internet egress, no SSH from the corporate network, limited IAM roles — without disrupting the rest of the system. It also makes logging and monitoring tractable because you know which flow logs and VPC flow logs belong to the CDE.\n\nA minimal security group setup for a CDE subnet might look like this (Terraform snippet, illustrative):\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"cde_api\" {\n  name        = \"cde-api\"\n  description = \"Allow only from app tier on 443\"\n  vpc_id      = aws_vpc.cde.id\n\n  ingress {\n    from_port       = 443\n    to_port         = 443\n    protocol        = \"tcp\"\n    security_groups = [aws_security_group.app_tier.id]\n  }\n\n  egress {\n    from_port   = 0\n    to_port     = 0\n    protocol    = \"-1\"\n    cidr_blocks = []\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe empty egress block is deliberate: no outbound traffic from the CDE except to specific internal endpoints (KMS, logging). This is a common trap: teams allow broad egress for package updates and then forget to lock it down, which violates the spirit of Requirement 1 and makes exfiltration easier.\n\n## Step 2 — Encrypt data at rest and in transit, but focus on key management\n\nRequirement 3 covers protection of stored cardholder data, and Requirement 4 covers encryption in transit. The architectural driver is not the encryption algorithm; it is key management. If you store encrypted PANs, you must have a documented key management process: key generation, distribution, rotation, retirement, and split knowledge. In AWS, this usually means using KMS customer managed keys (CMKs) with automatic rotation enabled where possible, and never storing keys alongside data.\n\nA typical pattern: the payment service calls KMS to encrypt a data key, then uses that data key with AES-256-GCM to encrypt the PAN. The encrypted data key is stored with the ciphertext. This is envelope encryption. The service's IAM role must have kms:Decrypt on the specific key, and that role should not be assumable by developers directly.\n\nWhy this shapes architecture: you cannot let the application tier decrypt PANs. Only the CDE service should have decrypt permission, and even then only for the specific key it needs. This forces a service boundary: the tokenization service or payment service becomes the only component with access to keys, and everything else works with tokens.\n\nA common failure mode: using a single KMS key for all environments and all data types. That makes rotation catastrophic because you cannot rotate without re-encrypting everything, and it violates least privilege. Instead, separate keys per environment and per data classification, and use key policies that deny decryption to anyone except the specific service role.\n\nIn transit, TLS 1.2+ is table stakes. The architectural point is to terminate TLS at the CDE edge and re-encrypt internally if traffic crosses subnets, or use mTLS between services. Many teams use a service mesh for mTLS, but that adds operational complexity; a simpler approach is to keep the CDE small enough that internal traffic stays within a single security group and never leaves the VPC.\n\n## Step 3 — Log everything, but protect the logs\n\nRequirement 10 is the one that most often forces a logging pipeline redesign. You must log all access to cardholder data, all administrative actions, and all authentication events, and you must protect those logs from modification. The architectural consequence: logs cannot live on the same host as the application, and they cannot be writable by the application user.\n\nA standard pattern is to ship logs to a centralized logging account or a dedicated log archive bucket with object lock (S3 Object Lock in compliance mode) and a retention policy. Use CloudTrail for API activity, VPC Flow Logs for network flows, and application logs for access events. The application should write logs to stdout, and a sidecar or agent forwards them; the application never has write access to the final store.\n\nWhy this matters: if an attacker compromises the CDE host, they should not be able to delete or alter logs. That means the log forwarder must use a separate IAM role with write-only permission to the log store, and the application role must not have s3:PutObject on the log bucket. A common trap is to grant the application role broad S3 permissions for other reasons, which accidentally gives it log deletion rights.\n\nA minimal log archive bucket policy (illustrative) denies deletion and requires encryption:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Sid\": \"DenyDelete\",\n      \"Effect\": \"Deny\",\n      \"Principal\": \"*\",\n      \"Action\": \"s3:DeleteObject\",\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::cde-logs/*\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"Sid\": \"RequireTLS\",\n      \"Effect\": \"Deny\",\n      \"Principal\": \"*\",\n      \"Action\": \"s3:*\",\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::cde-logs/*\",\n      \"Condition\": {\"Bool\": {\"aws:SecureTransport\": \"false\"}}\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nYou also need to log access to the logs themselves. CloudTrail data events on the log bucket give you that.\n\n## Step 4 — Secure the development process\n\nRequirement 6 covers secure development. The architectural driver: you need a CI/CD pipeline that enforces code review, separates duties, and prevents developers from deploying directly to production. In practice, that means infrastructure as code, peer review on every change, and a deployment pipeline that uses a separate role with no human access.\n\nA common pattern is to use a GitOps model: changes merge to main, a pipeline builds and tests, and a separate deployment role applies changes to the CDE. Developers never have console access to the CDE account; they have read-only access for debugging via a break-glass process that is logged and requires approval.\n\nWhy this shapes architecture: if developers can deploy directly, you cannot enforce separation of duties, and you cannot guarantee that only reviewed code reaches the CDE. This often forces a multi-account structure: a shared services account for CI/CD, a CDE account for production, and a development account for non-production. AWS Organizations with service control policies can prevent the CDE account from being used for anything except the pipeline role.\n\nA minimal SCP that denies all actions except from the pipeline role is complex; a simpler approach is to use IAM permissions boundaries on developer roles that deny sts:AssumeRole into the CDE account. This is a common trap: teams forget that a developer with admin in the dev account can still assume a role in the CDE account if the trust policy is too broad.\n\n## Step 5 — Tokenize early to reduce scope\n\nRequirement 3 also allows tokenization as a way to reduce scope. The architectural decision: if you can replace the PAN with a token before it hits your systems, those systems are out of scope. This is often the highest-leverage change. A tokenization service sits at the edge, receives the PAN from the payment form (via a JavaScript integration that posts directly to the tokenization provider), and returns a token. Your application never sees the PAN.\n\nIf you build your own tokenization, the token vault becomes the most sensitive part of the system and must be in the CDE. The architectural pattern is to keep the vault small, with a narrow API, and to use format-preserving encryption only if downstream systems require it. Most teams are better off using a payment processor's tokenization (e.g., Stripe, Adyen) to avoid building and auditing a vault.\n\nWhy this matters: scope reduction is the difference between a 6-month audit and a 6-week one. It also changes the blast radius: if the application is compromised, the attacker gets tokens, not PANs.\n\n## Step 6 — Monitor and test the boundary\n\nRequirement 11 covers security testing. The architectural driver: you need continuous monitoring of the CDE boundary and regular penetration testing. In AWS, that means GuardDuty, Security Hub, and Config rules that alert on changes to security groups, IAM policies, or encryption settings. You also need file integrity monitoring on CDE hosts, which can be done with a host-based agent or with immutable infrastructure (e.g., container images that are replaced, not patched).\n\nA common failure mode is to treat monitoring as an afterthought and only enable it after an incident. Instead, build it into the CDE from day one: enable VPC Flow Logs, CloudTrail, and GuardDuty in the CDE account, and route findings to a security account. Use AWS Config rules to detect public S3 buckets or unencrypted volumes.\n\n## How to verify it works\n\nYou can test the segmentation by attempting to reach the CDE from the application tier on a port that should be closed. Use `nc -zv <cde-host> 22` from an app-tier instance; it should time out. Check that the application role cannot decrypt the KMS key by attempting `aws kms decrypt` with a ciphertext from the CDE; it should return AccessDenied. Verify that logs are immutable by attempting to delete an object from the log bucket with the application role; it should fail.\n\nFor the pipeline, attempt to push directly to the CDE account from a developer role; it should be denied. These tests are simple but catch the most common misconfigurations.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**What is the minimum scope for PCI DSS?**\n\nThe minimum scope is any system that stores, processes, or transmits cardholder data, plus any system that can impact the security of the CDE. In practice, that includes the CDE network, the tokenization service if self-hosted, the logging pipeline, and the CI/CD pipeline that deploys to the CDE. You can reduce scope by outsourcing tokenization and using a payment processor's hosted payment page.\n\n**Do I need a separate VPC for PCI compliance?**\n\nNot strictly, but it is the simplest way to demonstrate segmentation. A separate VPC with strict security groups and no internet egress makes it easy to show that the CDE is isolated. If you use subnets within a shared VPC, you must prove that security groups and NACLs prevent lateral movement, which is harder to audit.\n\n**How often should I rotate keys?**\n\nPCI DSS requires key rotation but does not prescribe a frequency. A common practice is annual rotation for keys that encrypt data at rest, and rotation on compromise. Using KMS automatic rotation (which rotates the backing key annually) satisfies the requirement for many cases, but you should document your rotation policy and test it.\n\n**Can I store logs in the same account as the CDE?**\n\nYou can, but it is risky because an attacker with admin in the CDE account could delete logs. A better pattern is to send logs to a separate logging account with a restrictive bucket policy that denies deletion even to the CDE account's root. This is a common audit finding.\n\n## Where to go from here\n\nStart by drawing your current CDE boundary on a whiteboard. Then check one thing: whether your application role has permission to decrypt the KMS key that protects card data. If it does, you have a scope and least-privilege problem to fix before your next audit. Open your IAM policy for the application role and search for `kms:Decrypt`; if it is there, remove it and move decryption to a dedicated service.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
